# Nexus Design System

Documentation site for the Nexus Design System, built with **Astro**.

## Structure
```
src/
  styles/        → base.css (variables, reset), layout.css (sidebar, shell), components.css (UI pieces)
  layouts/       → Default.astro (shared sidebar + page shell, wraps every page)
  pages/         → one .astro file per section (file-based routing)
  tokens/        → JSON source-of-truth for design token values
  components/    → reusable Astro components
.local/          → updates.md (decision log), experiments, internal docs
```

## Sections
- **Foundations:** Colours, Typography, Spacing, Grid
- **Components:** Atoms, Molecules, Organisms

## Key Convention
- **British English everywhere** — display text AND URLs and filenames (e.g. `colours.astro` → `/colours`, not `colors`)

## Storybook & Chromatic
- Storybook is the primary place to exercise components in isolation. When adding or updating atoms/molecules/organisms, prefer to add or update Storybook stories alongside Astro docs.
- Local Storybook: use `npm run storybook` (port 6006) for day-to-day component work; the Astro site remains the primary documentation surface.
- Chromatic is the hosted Storybook + visual regression service. It is wired via the `chromatic` npm script and `.github/workflows/chromatic.yml`.
- CI: on every push/PR to `main`, GitHub Actions runs `npm run chromatic -- --exit-zero-on-changes --exit-once-uploaded`. Treat Chromatic as the source of truth for visual diffs; GitHub Actions status should remain green unless the build itself fails.
- Do not commit `storybook-static/` to the repo. Hosted Storybook lives on Chromatic; link to it from docs if needed instead of checking in build artefacts.

## Session Continuity
Always check `.local/updates.md` for the full decision log before starting new work.
